WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.735
A fee job divides 8 by each amount: 1, 2, 0 and 4.

2
00:00:06.735 --> 00:00:11.012
Time runs left to right, and each marble is one item.

3
00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:12.346
8 over 1 is 8.

4
00:00:12.346 --> 00:00:13.458
8 over 2 is 4.

5
00:00:13.458 --> 00:00:18.067
Then the 0 throws, and an error signal goes down the line.

6
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:19.815
What happens to the 4?

7
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:23.211
Nothing.

8
00:00:23.211 --> 00:00:24.814
An error is terminal.

9
00:00:24.814 --> 00:00:28.401
It ended the source, so the 4 is never divided.

10
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:34.742
onErrorResume catches the error on its way down, and switches to a fallback, like cached fees.

11
00:00:34.742 --> 00:00:37.017
It replaces the rest of the stream.

12
00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:38.381
It doesn't resume it.

13
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.279
Now an issuer that keeps failing.

14
00:00:41.279 --> 00:00:44.277
When the error reaches retry, it subscribes again.

15
00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:49.133
The subscription travels up to the source, and the source calls the issuer again.

16
00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:56.413
retry(3) does that three times, in the same millisecond: four calls, then the original error comes through.

17
00:00:56.633 --> 00:01:01.294
Retry.backoff(3, 100 milliseconds) spreads them out.

18
00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:11.153
With jitter off, the calls go at 0, 100, 300 and 700 milliseconds, then it fails with RetryExhaustedException.

19
00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:18.022
This client calls the issuer when you call the method, and wraps the stored answer in Mono.error.

20
00:01:18.022 --> 00:01:20.307
How many calls reach the issuer now?

21
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:23.741
Just one.

22
00:01:23.741 --> 00:01:29.442
Each retry re-reads the stored error, because nothing runs at subscription time.

23
00:01:29.442 --> 00:01:32.933
Mono.defer makes every subscription a fresh call.

24
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:38.882
An issuer that never answers sends no error, so retry never fires.

25
00:01:38.882 --> 00:01:45.069
timeout(500 milliseconds) turns that silence into an error, and cancels the slow call.

26
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.597
Put it above retryWhen, and each new attempt gets its own 500 milliseconds.

27
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:57.864
In Java and in Kotlin: a fresh call per attempt, a timeout per attempt, and a filtered backoff.

28
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:02.283
So an error ends a stream for good.

29
00:02:02.283 --> 00:02:04.597
Error operators replace what's left.

30
00:02:04.597 --> 00:02:09.739
retry subscribes again from the source, and timeout turns silence into an error.

31
00:02:09.967 --> 00:02:10.847
Quick check.

32
00:02:10.847 --> 00:02:17.080
Retry.backoff(2, 50 milliseconds), jitter off, against a lazy call that always fails.

33
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.187
When is the issuer called, and when does the chain fail?
